## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of crash-consistent data structures, persistent memory programming, and low-latency recovery mechanisms in real-world systems, connecting hardware flush instructions to correct ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Non-volatile memory semantics and persistence instructions (clwb, sfence)
- Write-ahead logging protocols and commit/rollback mechanisms for crash safety
- Epoch-based reclamation and idempotent recovery procedures
- Log replay and in-memory index reconstruction after power loss
- NUMA-aware memory layout and efficient log space management

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of log append and commit protocol
typedef struct {
    uint64_t key;
    uint64_t val;
    uint64_t epoch;
    uint8_t committed; // 0=pending, 1=committed
} log_entry_t;

// Put operation with crash consistency
int kv_put(uint64_t key, uint64_t val) {
    log_entry_t *entry = alloc_log_entry();
    entry->key = key;
    entry->val = val;
    entry->epoch = get_current_epoch();
    entry->committed = 0; // pending

    _mm_clwb(entry); // flush to NVRAM
    _mm_sfence();    // ensure ordering

    entry->committed = 1; // mark committed
    _mm_clwb(&entry->committed);
    _mm_sfence();

    update_index(key, val); // update in-memory index
    return 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level synchronization primitives, memory ordering, and ABA avoidance in a tiny mailbox primitive.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic CAS on a 64-bit slot; sequence counters prevent ABA.
- Memory_order_release on publish, memory_order_acquire on receive for visibility.
- Fill-empty state via payload==0 heuristic for simplicity; robust designs can reserve a dedicated marker.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { _Atomic uint64_t slot; } Mailbox;
void mailbox_init(Mailbox *m){ atomic_init(&m->slot, 0); }
bool mailbox_send(Mailbox *m, uint32_t payload){
  uint64_t cur = atomic_load_explicit(&m->slot, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t seq = (uint32_t)(cur>>32);
  uint64_t want = ((uint64_t)seq<<32);
  uint64_t next = (((uint64_t)(seq+1))<<32) | payload;
  return atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&m->slot, &want, next);
}
bool mailbox_recv(Mailbox *m, uint32_t *out){
  uint64_t cur = atomic_load_explicit(&m->slot, memory_order_acquire);
  uint32_t seq = (uint32_t)(cur>>32);
  uint32_t payload = (uint32_t)(cur & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  if (payload == 0) return false;
  uint64_t next = (((uint64_t)(seq+1))<<32) | 0u;
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&m->slot, &cur, next)){
    *out = payload; return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

